---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { heroPhoto } from "../data/gallery";
import { directionsUrl, primaryPhone } from "../data/contact";

/**
 * The entrance to the home page: a full-width photograph, the `h1` on it, and under that a
 * welcoming sentence and three things to do.
 *
 * Up to version 0.5 this was a centred column of text on a cream ground. The holding
 * supplied its own photographs in September, and the home page showed not one of them above
 * the fold - the first photograph on it was the thumbnail in a season card.
 *
 * ## Why this photograph in particular
 *
 * Because it is landscape. That is the only criterion this frame has to meet, and the only
 * one no amount of code can make up for. The first version of this band took `tunnelPhoto` -
 * a portrait 1500x2000 frame, like 22 of the 23 photographs from the session. At
 * `sizes="100vw"` that put a ceiling of 1500px of real detail on a photograph that needs
 * 2800px on a 1400px monitor at 2x, and cropped the middle quarter out of its height along
 * the way. Up to version 0.7 `heroPhoto` was gallery-19 (2000x1500), the only landscape
 * frame in the holding's photo session; in September 2026 it was replaced, on an explicit
 * instruction, with a generated image (1448x1086, the same 4:3) - see the comment beside
 * `heroPhoto` in `src/data/gallery.ts` for why that is out of keeping with the rest of that
 * file. The rule for the future is short: **only a landscape frame goes in this band**, and
 * if the owners ever shoot a real panorama, `heroPhoto` is swapped and nothing else.
 *
 * The width ceiling in `widths` tracks the actual file behind `heroPhoto.src` - deliberately,
 * rather than `2400px that do not exist`: Astro trims the list to the file's width anyway, so
 * a larger value adds nothing but an illusion. With the current file that is 1448px.
 *
 * ## One crop, not two
 *
 * `width`/`height` **must** match the original's ratio. When they did not (1600x686 against
 * a 3:4 file), Astro cropped the photograph to 2.33:1 while generating the variants, and
 * `cover` in CSS cropped that result a second time to 3:1 - two crops, neither of which
 * could be aimed. Now Astro only scales, all the cropping is done by `object-fit`, and
 * `object-position` aims it.
 *
 * ## The scrim
 *
 * The `h1` lies on the photograph, so legibility depends on the darkening (--hero-scrim)
 * rather than on luck in the frame. The scrim's top padding is part of that calculation, not
 * a spacing, and it works the opposite way round to intuition: the scrim is anchored to the
 * bottom, so **more** padding makes it taller and pushes the text onto a lower fraction of
 * its height, which is denser alpha. In the 3:1 frame the padding is shorter (2.75rem rather
 * than 5.5rem) so the gradient fits inside the photograph - and that is why the slope in
 * --hero-scrim is denser to compensate. Those two numbers are one calculation spread across
 * two files: changing either one requires recomputing the other.
 *
 * The critical element is the eyebrow above the heading, not the heading: it sits highest, so
 * it gets the thinnest part of the gradient, and at 10.5px it needs 4.5:1 where the heading
 * at 46px needs 3:1. Two versions running passed on the heading and failed on the eyebrow.
 *
 * Computed for gallery-19 against the brightest pixel it contained - pure white
 * (255,255,255) in a gap in the glasshouse, which is the worst case there is: the eyebrow at
 * 5.3:1 with an alpha of 0.77, the heading at 7.8-11.6:1 lower down the slope. Changing the
 * photograph, the scrim's padding or the length of the heading calls for measuring the
 * **eyebrow** again.
 *
 * September's swap to a generated image was not measured in a real browser - the screenshot
 * tool was unavailable in that session. An offline estimate (a 3:1 crop at
 * `object-position: center 42%`, the same four-stop gradient, a scrim box of ~237px at a
 * width of ~1440px) came out at ~5.1:1 for the eyebrow and ~8.4:1 for the heading - the same
 * neighbourhood as the old numbers, so nothing points to a regression, but it is an
 * approximation at one screen width, not a measurement. **Confirm it in a browser**,
 * especially at the widths where the band is 3:2 rather than 3:1.
 *
 * `loading="eager"` and `fetchpriority="high"` - this is the home page's LCP and there is no
 * point pretending otherwise.
 */
---

<section class="intro">
  <div class="intro__hero">
    <Picture
      class="intro__photo"
      src={heroPhoto.src}
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="jpg"
      widths={[960, 1400]}
      sizes="100vw"
      width={1448}
      height={1086}
      alt={heroPhoto.alt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="intro__scrim">
      <p class="intro__place">Cholewianka pod Kazimierzem Dolnym</p>
      <h1 class="intro__title">Kwiaty balkonowe, rabatowe i chryzantemy z własnej uprawy</h1>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="intro__below">
    <p class="intro__body">
      Zachęcamy do zapoznania się z naszą ofertą. Zamówienia można składać telefonicznie lub na
      terenie naszego gospodarstwa w Cholewiance pod Kazimierzem Dolnym. Wszystko, co sprzedajemy,
      pochodzi z naszej własnej uprawy.
    </p>
    <div class="intro__actions">
      <a class="cta" href={primaryPhone.href}>Zadzwoń: {primaryPhone.display}</a>
      <a class="cta-line" href="/kwiaty-balkonowe/">Zobacz ofertę</a>
      <a class="cta-line" href={directionsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wyznacz trasę</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .intro {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* The photograph and the scrim lie in one grid cell, both as grid items - the scrim is
     not absolutely positioned. That is a correctness condition, not a style: the scrim is
     anchored to the bottom and its height comes from the length of the heading, not from
     the frame. Below ~390px the heading breaks onto enough lines that the scrim is taller
     than the 3:2 frame and - as an absolute element - escaped above the photograph, laying
     a grey smear on the paper over it (11px at 360px, 91px at 320px). Clipping it with
     `overflow: hidden` is a trap here: it then cuts off the eyebrow and the top of the
     heading. As a grid item the scrim helps determine the band's height, so the frame grows
     to fit the text instead of the text escaping the frame. The gradient's density and the
     scrim's padding are untouched - the eyebrow and heading measurements are the same. */
  .intro__hero {
    display: grid;
    /* An explicit column rather than the default `auto`: an `auto` track measures the
       max-content of its items, and since the scrim became a grid item its max-content (an
       unbroken heading plus the eyebrow) pushed the band out to 520px on a 320px screen and
       gave the whole page a horizontal scrollbar. `minmax(0, 1fr)` pins the column to the
       container's width and lets the content break. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  /* <Picture> renders <picture><img>; the grid item is the wrapper, so the frame sits on
     it. `min-height: 100%` grows to the row when the scrim turns out taller than the frame;
     `align-self: stretch` alone does not work here, because an element with `aspect-ratio`
     does not stretch, and `height: 100%` would push the width out by the ratio. */
  .intro__hero > :global(picture) {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 100%;
    /* On a phone, 3:1 leaves a band of photograph about as tall as a finger; 3:2 gives a
       frame there in which you can see what is in the tunnel. The only media query in the
       project, and a deliberate one: this is a choice of crop, not of layout, so auto-fit
       cannot settle it. */
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
  }

  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .intro__hero > :global(picture) {
      aspect-ratio: 3 / 1;
      /* A 3:1 panorama drops below the height the scrim needs for the eyebrow and the h1 all
         by itself - without this lower bound the gradient fills the whole frame and nothing
         is left of the photograph. The bound is computed against the tallest content of the
         scrim, not against "the look". `max(100%, ...)` rather than the bound alone, so
         growing to the row stays in force.

         A side effect of the bound: between 700px and ~1150px the band is wider than the
         3:1 the ratio calls for (at 700px it works out at ~1.75:1), because the bound holds
         the height. That is deliberate - below 400px the scrim eats the whole photograph -
         but it means that in this range *more* of the photograph is visible, not less, so
         `object-position` does not run away downwards. */
      min-height: max(100%, clamp(400px, 34vw, 560px));
    }
  }

  .intro__photo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    /* Beats the `height: auto` that global.css puts on every img. */
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* The only crop of the photograph in this band, so it is aimed rather than left in the
       middle. The scrim is anchored to the bottom and at 3:1 takes most of the height, so
       the photograph reads mainly through the top of the frame - 42% pulls the hanging
       baskets and the variety labels up into that visible band instead of leaving the
       glasshouse roof there. At 3:2 on a phone the frame is almost complete (4:3 -> 3:2 is
       88% of the height) and the value moves nothing that matters. */
    object-position: center 42%;
  }

  .intro__scrim {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    align-self: end;
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.75rem) var(--edge) clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    background: var(--hero-scrim);
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  /* A label for the place, not a substitute for the address in the text: the name of the
     village also stands in the paragraph below, because 10.5px of uppercase on a photograph
     is a poor sole hiding place for the most important fact on the site of a holding people
     drive to - and text on an image weighs less in a search engine than a sentence in
     prose. */
  .intro__place {
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--green-lit);
  }

  .intro__title {
    font-size: var(--text-h1);
    line-height: 1.08;
    color: var(--paper);
    max-width: 20ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .intro__below {
    padding: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem) var(--edge) clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.15rem;
  }

  .intro__body {
    max-width: 56ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .intro__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.7rem 1.4rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
</style>
